  "summary": "A traditional log building technique where logs are scribed to fit tightly together without nails or metal fasteners.",
  "description": "Features rounded logs with interlocking corners and a distinctive overhang at each level; developed using abundant forest resources and adapting to cold climates. The scribing technique creates a tight seal without gaps, providing excellent insulation.",
  "culturalContext": "Lafté — Norwegian laftebygg — is Scandinavia's medieval log-building tradition: round logs scribed into saddled notches so walls settle tight without nails, from stave-church precursors to 18th-century seter cabins and storehouses. Migrants carried it across the American frontier Midwest, and today Norwegian conservation carpenters still teach scribe-fit notching as living heritage.",
